<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.description is capped at 255 characters. The full rationale below is
        operationally important, so it lives here rather than being trimmed; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. It is INSIDE the root element on
        purpose - a comment ABOVE <ValidationRule> breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with the
        misleading error "unable to find matching parent xml file". Same shape as the house
        precedent, Contract_Signed_Before_Closed_Won, and its sibling on Lead.

        =====================================================================
        WHAT IT DOES AND WHY IT EXISTS HERE NOW (WS2, 2026-08-30).
        =====================================================================
        FSD Gap Tranche 1 §26 asks for "Broker First-Seen Timestamp is system-set and cannot
        be edited", naming Opportunity.Broker_First_Seen__c. When the sibling Lead rule
        (Lead.Broker_First_Seen_Is_System_Set) was built on 2026-08-16, a repo-wide grep
        found NOTHING wrote this Opportunity field - no Apex writer, no Flow writer - so a
        rule built here at that time would have permanently locked an already-blank field.
        The rule was built on Lead.First_Seen_Date__c instead, as a deliberate, user-approved
        re-target (see that file's own header for the full account).

        WS2 changes the premise: LeadConvertService.applyDealFields now copies
        Lead.First_Seen_Date__c into Opportunity.Broker_First_Seen__c on every conversion, so
        this field is live for the first time. The FSD-named field now has a real writer, so
        the rule the FSD originally asked for is built HERE, on the field it originally named,
        alongside (not instead of) the Lead rule, which still protects Lead.First_Seen_Date__c
        for Leads that are never converted or are still mid-funnel.

        =====================================================================
        THE FORMULA AND WHY EACH WRITER IS OR IS NOT CAUGHT.
        =====================================================================
        AND(ISCHANGED(Broker_First_Seen__c), NOT(ISBLANK(PRIORVALUE(Broker_First_Seen__c))))

        🔴 CORRECTED 2026-08-30 (P7/WS6 admin pass) - THE PRECEDING VERSION OF THIS BLOCK
        WRONGLY CLAIMED "ISCHANGED() is FALSE on record creation... this rule CANNOT fire on
        insert." That is FALSE FOR THIS OBJECT and must not be re-introduced.

        LeadConvertService.applyDealFields does NOT insert the Opportunity. By the time it
        runs, Database.convertLead has already created the Opportunity; applyDealFields
        mutates the already-existing record `o` in memory and adds it to the `updates` list,
        which the service commits with a single `update updates;` (an UPDATE DML, not an
        INSERT). ISCHANGED(Broker_First_Seen__c) is therefore TRUE on that write - the field
        genuinely transitions from blank to a value on an update, not a creation.

        THE RULE IS SAFE HERE ONLY BECAUSE OF THE SECOND CLAUSE,
        NOT(ISBLANK(PRIORVALUE(Broker_First_Seen__c))). PRIORVALUE is blank on every first
        conversion (the field has never been set before), so the second clause evaluates
        FALSE, the AND() is FALSE, and the rule does not fire - not because ISCHANGED was
        false, but because the prior value was blank. Writer-by-writer:

        - Lead conversion (LeadConvertService.applyDealFields -> `update updates`): ISCHANGED
          = TRUE, PRIORVALUE = blank -> second clause FALSE -> rule does not fire. Safe by the
          PRIORVALUE clause, not by ISCHANGED.
        - Manual edit / inline edit / API write / Data Loader change to an ALREADY-STAMPED
          Opportunity: ISCHANGED = TRUE, PRIORVALUE = populated -> both clauses TRUE -> rule
          fires, and correctly - this is the population the rule exists to protect once
          conversion has set it.
        - Seed scripts that write this field directly (scripts/seed-deal.apex,
          scripts/seed-fsd-06-volume-pipeline.apex) perform a genuine INSERT, so ISCHANGED is
          FALSE there and the rule cannot fire regardless of PRIORVALUE - this is the only
          writer for which the retired "insert" reasoning was ever actually true.

        WHY THE DISTINCTION MATTERS ENOUGH TO CORRECT: a future reader who "simplified" this
        formula to ISCHANGED(Broker_First_Seen__c) alone - on the retired comment's own stated
        reasoning that conversion is an insert and therefore already exempt - would produce a
        formula that is FALSE for this writer too, since conversion is in fact an UPDATE and
        ISCHANGED is TRUE on it. That simplified rule WOULD fire on every Lead conversion, and
        because `update updates` in LeadConvertService is deliberately ALL-OR-NONE, one
        validation-rule failure rolls back every Opportunity in that `update updates` call -
        i.e. every conversion in the chunk, not just the one that tripped it.

        =====================================================================
        BYPASS: DELIBERATELY NONE, MIRRORING THE LEAD RULE'S ACCEPTED DECISION.
        =====================================================================
        The rule's entire purpose is anti-tampering. An admin bypass re-opens the field for
        exactly the population most able to tamper with it, and a genuine data correction has
        an auditable path already (deactivate this rule, correct the value, reactivate). If a
        bypass is ever added, it MUST be a Custom Permission checked via
        {!$Permission.CustomPermission.<Name>} - never a reference to a User field. A rule
        bound to a field evaluates FALSE for anyone lacking FLS READ on that field, silently,
        with no error and no log.

        =====================================================================
        FLS AND THIS RULE ARE COMPLEMENTARY, NOT ALTERNATIVES.
        =====================================================================
        Removing Broker_First_Seen__c edit FLS from a profile/permission set stops that
        persona from editing it in the UI, but does nothing about a Data Loader run or an API
        call by a user who DOES hold the FLS grant. This validation rule is what closes that
        gap; it runs in system context regardless of the running user's FLS.
    -->
    <fullName>Broker_First_Seen_Is_System_Set</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks changing Broker_First_Seen__c once stamped at Lead conversion. Starts the broker protection window (BP_Expiry__c); window length comes from Broker_Protection_Config__mdt, not a literal. Must not be hand-edited afterward. See XML comment.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    ISCHANGED(Broker_First_Seen__c),
    NOT(ISBLANK(PRIORVALUE(Broker_First_Seen__c)))
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorDisplayField>Broker_First_Seen__c</errorDisplayField>
    <errorMessage>Broker First Seen is set automatically at Lead conversion and cannot be changed afterward. It drives the broker protection window.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
